th  the  organic flow force it works very well  with a fluorescent proteins as you've  seen here  we almost use exclusively fluorescent  proteins in in cells because it's much  easier to label for us again  so that's all you need and speaking of  labels do we need special labels  well yeah so as i just tried to indicate  um so  uh of course organic dyes are uh give  you a better signal to noise ratio so we  have beautiful knife  curves on artificial biolayers but the  moment you go into a living  system but a cell or or a small organism  uh it is much easier to use fluorescent  proteins as labels  and we have used the right range of  different solution proteins from m  cherry to egfp  so uh they seem to work all all quite  well and they get  same results as we typically get this uh  fcs con focus yes  thank you and we'll wrap with this final  question and we'll go with this one  because it's come in a few times what  about dual color  fccs  yes that can be done as well we have at  the moment showing it only with turf  so we have measured for example where  the egfr dimerization which we have  shown here with nmd we have shown that  before  as well with imaging fccs  we are working at the moment to do this  as well with a light sheet microscopy  thank you tristan now before we go i do  want to thank our audience for joining  us today and for their interesting  questions  again there are quite a few questions we  are unable to answer they will be  answered via the email address that you  provided  i would like to once again thank torson  woolland and dr alan mullen for their  time today  we'd also like to thank labrutz and our  sponsor andor for underwriting today's  educational webcast this webcast can be  viewed on demand  labyrinths will alert you via email when  it is available for replay  in that email there will also be a  downloadable link a link where you can  download your  certificate of participation we  encourage you also to share that email  with your col